    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for explaining that. so just to be sure i get it, when you say the ldl \"builds up\" in the artery walls, is it like it's actively attacking them or just sitting there? i'm trying to visualize what's actually happening inside the blood vessels.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "LDL cholesterol doesn't actively \"attack\" your arteries; instead, it can infiltrate the vessel walls through tiny gaps and accumulate there over time. This accumulation triggers a unique chemical reaction where immune cells called macrophages try to clean up the excess, but they become overwhelmed and transform into soapy, sticky cells known as \"foam cells.\" When these foam cells accumulate and die, they leave behind a build-up that hardens over time into plaque, which narrows the artery and restricts blood flow.",
      "source": "target",
